  "text": "We have previously published two  THA. The first study large register-based cohort studies on cardiovascular risk factors inindicated a higher risk of cardiovascular morbidity and mortality in patients with THA than in those without, eight years or more after index surgery (15). The second showed a higher risk of CVD in patients revised for osteolysis than in patients without revision surgery (16). Periacetabular osteolysis is difficult to study because it is “silent”, lacking initial symptoms such as pain, and takes many years, if ever, to develop. Osteolysis and atherosclerosis may share a common denominator: long-standing low-grade inflammation (18, 37). Here we examined the association between osteolysis, CVE and risk markers at the individual level in a clinical setting. A similar pattern was reported by Gordon et al. (15), who in a nationwide Swedish matched cohort of 91,527 patients operated with THA and 270,688 controls (matched 1:3 on sex, age and residence, mean follow-up 10 years) found that cardiovascular mortality was lower than in controls during the first 5 to 9 years but exceeded that of controls after 8.8 years (95% CI 7.0–10.5), with a hazard ratio of 1.11 (95% CI 1.05–1.17) between 9 and 13 years postoperatively and a rate ratio of 1.08 (95% CI 1.06–1.11) for cardiovascular re-admissions."
